## Workflow (the standard change flow)

```
   <git-host>/<git-user>  ·  <your-repo-name>   (single source of truth)
   │
   ├─►  DEV CLONE (where you edit):  <edit + commit + push; config manager never reads here>
   │
   └─►  PROD SOURCE (each node):  <config manager's source dir; update = pull + apply>
```

For a config change: edit in the dev clone → (approval) commit + push → on each affected node,
pull the prod source → **backup** → **diff** → (approval) **apply** → verify.

## Remote-node access (if multi-node)

- Reach nodes over the private mesh + key-only SSH (`guide/08-networking.md`). Read-only
  inventory runs freely; **material changes on a remote node still pause for approval** (Rule 1),
  same as local.
- **Never handle passwords.** Anything needing an interactive password/unlock is handed back to
  the user; the operator does not attempt it.

## File scope (what the operator may edit)

- Docs, plans, status (`<docs-dir>/**`, `<registry-file>`, this file).
- The config-manager source (dotfiles, install scripts) — only as approved per change.
- The status view source (`<dashboard-or-status-doc>`).

Restricted (do not modify without explicit instruction):
- `.git/`
- SSH config/keys, credential/auth directories (sensitive — `guide/06-secrets.md`).
- Anything holding secrets.

## Memory / status

Project-scoped status and history live in `<registry-file>` + `<docs-dir>/<project>/`. This
file holds durable behavioral rules only.
